%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[c]{}
\Large
\textcolor{Maroon}{Example adapted from:}
\\~\
\\~\
\centering
\textcolor{Maroon}{Chattopadhyay, Raghabendra \& Duflo, Esther (2004). Women as policy makers: Evidence from a randomized policy experiment in India.}
\\~\
as discussed in 
\\~\
\textcolor{Maroon}{Gerber, Alan S. \& Green, Donald P. (2012). Field experiments: Design, analysis, and interpretation.}
\nocite{chattopadhyay2004women, gerber2012field, butler2011politicians}


\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item We start with a tiny randomized experiment so we can see every moving part (potential outcomes, treatment assignment, estimand vs estimator, uncertainty).
\item The numbers in the table are simplified to make the inference ideas transparent; the goal is the logic, not the specific study details.
\end{itemize}

}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Chattopadhyay \& Duflo example}

\begin{itemize}
\item Population: 7 villages\pause
\item Treatment: $W = 1$ if female-headed council, $W = 0$ if male \pause
\item Outcome: Budget allocation to sanitation\pause
\end{itemize}

\begin{table}[]
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
& $Y_i(0)$ & $Y_i(1)$ & $W_i$ & $Y_i$ & $\tau_i$ \\
Village 1 & 10 & 15 & 1 & 15 & 5 \\
Village 2 & 15 & 15 & 0 & 15 & 0 \\
Village 3 & 20 & 30 & 0 & 20 & 10 \\
Village 4 & 20 & 15 & 0 & 20 & -5 \\
Village 5 & 10 & 20 & 0 & 10 & 10 \\
Village 6 & 15 & 15 & 0 & 15 & 0 \\
Village 7 & 15 & 30 & 1 & 30 & 15 \\
{\textbf{Average}} & {\textbf{15}} & {\textbf{20}} & & &{\textbf{5}}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

$$
\text{ATE} = {\E[\tau_i]=5}
$$

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item This is the ``God's-eye view'': we can see $Y_i(0)$ and $Y_i(1)$ for every village.
\item Individual treatment effect: $\tau_i = Y_i(1)-Y_i(0)$. The ATE is the average of these $\tau_i$'s (here, 5).
\item In real life we never observe both potential outcomes for the same unit (the fundamental problem of causal inference).
\end{itemize}

}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Chattopadhyay \& Duflo example}

What we actually see:

\begin{table}[]
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
& $Y_i(0)$ & $Y_i(1)$ & $W_i$ & $Y_i$ & $\tau_i$ \\
Village 1 & \textcolor{red}{?} & 15 & 1 & 15 & \textcolor{red}{?} \\
Village 2 & 15 & \textcolor{red}{?} & 0 & 15 & \textcolor{red}{?} \\
Village 3 & 20 & \textcolor{red}{?} & 0 & 20 & \textcolor{red}{?} \\
Village 4 & 20 & \textcolor{red}{?} & 0 & 20 & \textcolor{red}{?} \\
Village 5 & 10 & \textcolor{red}{?} & 0 & 10 & \textcolor{red}{?} \\
Village 6 & 15 & \textcolor{red}{?} & 0 & 15 & \textcolor{red}{?} \\
Village 7 & \textcolor{red}{?} & 30 & 1 & 30 & \textcolor{red}{?}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}
\pause
To produce an \textit{estimate} of the ATE, we can compare people who received treatment 1 to people who received treatment 0.
\pause
$$
\bar{Y}(1) = 22.5 \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \bar{Y}(0) = 16
$$
\pause
$$
\hat{\tau} = 22.5 - 16 = \bm{6.5}
$$


\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Emphasize the missing counterfactuals: each unit reveals only one potential outcome, so $\tau_i$ is never directly observed.
\item Difference-in-means is a natural estimator of the ATE under random assignment: treated average minus control average.
\item Here the estimated ATE is 6.5, which differs from the true ATE (5) because of random assignment (sampling variation from randomization).
\end{itemize}

}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Estimand}

The \textit{estimand} is the parameter of interest--it is the quantity that we would like to know about.

\pause

For example, when we care about causal effects, the estimand may be:

\begin{itemize}
\item the Average Treatment Effect (ATE)
\item  the Average effect of Treatment on the Treated (ATT)
\item  the Average effect of Treatment on the Control (ATC)
\end{itemize}

\pause

Why might these three quantities differ?

\pause


\textit{Notational aside: we often denote the estimand with the greek letter $\theta$. Specific estimands may have conventional notations, such as $\tau$ for the ATE, $\mu$ for the mean, or $\sigma$ for the standard deviation.}


\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item An estimand is what we want to know about the world (a target parameter), not what we can compute from the sample.
\item ATE vs ATT vs ATC differ when treatment effects are heterogeneous and/or when treatment assignment is correlated with potential outcomes.
\item Good habit: name the estimand \emph{before} picking an estimator.
\end{itemize}

}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Estimator}

An \textit{estimator} is a function of the data we observe; it is a statistic that gives us an informed guess about the value of the estimand. \\~\
\pause 
Below, the estimator is the function $g(\cdot)$. \\~\

$$
g(X_1, \dots, X_n)
$$
\pause
We can also think of it as a recipe. Given some data, $X_1, \dots, X_n$, follow the instructions $g(\cdot)$ to produce an \textbf{estimate}.

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Estimator = rule/algorithm that maps data to a number (a random variable because it depends on random data/assignment).
\item Distinguish: estimand (fixed but unknown) vs estimator (random) vs estimate (one realized value).
\item We'll use difference-in-means as our running estimator for the ATE.
\end{itemize}

}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Estimate}

An \textit{estimate} is what we calculate from our estimator with a specific set of data. Below, the estimate is the quantity $\hat{\theta}_n$. 

$$
\hat{\theta}_n = g(X_1, \dots, X_n)
$$

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item The estimate is what you compute \emph{this time} from the observed data.
\item If we repeated the random assignment (or re-sampled), we would get different estimates even if the true estimand stays the same.
\end{itemize}

}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}


\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth,height=0.8\textheight,keepaspectratio]{../assets/eee.jpg}
\end{figure}

(image cred: \@simongrund89)


\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Quick mnemonic: estimand = what you want; estimator = how you compute; estimate = what you got.
\item We'll keep coming back to this whenever we talk about uncertainty and inference.
\end{itemize}

}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Difference-in-means}

Proposed estimator for $\E[\tau_i]$: compare people who received treatment $1$ to people who received treatment $0$. \\~\

\pause
This is the difference in means estimator:

$$
\hat\tau_{DM} = \frac{\sum_i^n Y_i W_i}{\sum_i^n W_i} - \frac{\sum_i^n Y_i (1-W_i)}{\sum_i^n (1-W_i)}
$$

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Write $\hat\tau_{DM}=\bar Y_1-\bar Y_0$; the formula here is just the algebraic way to compute those means.
\item Under random assignment, this is an unbiased estimator of the ATE; the big question is how variable it is.
\end{itemize}

}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%


\begin{frame}{Chattopadhyay \& Duflo example}

\begin{itemize}
\item We have produced an estimate here, by taking the difference in means. \pause 
\item But what can we say about our uncertainty about this estimate? The number of observations in each group is pretty small.\pause
\item Is the estimate meaningfully different from zero? \pause
\item How likely would we be to see an effect this size just by chance?
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Transition: we have an estimate, but we need a principled way to quantify uncertainty.
\item With only 2 treated villages here, random assignment can produce ``weird'' splits; the estimate might swing a lot.
\item This motivates hypothesis testing / randomization inference: what would we see \emph{by chance} under a null?
\end{itemize}

}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Chattopadhyay \& Duflo example}

\begin{itemize}
\item One way to think about this is to assume the individual treatment effect for all individuals is exactly zero. Then, no matter how we randomized treatment assignment, we would see the same $Y_i$s.\pause
\item We can then say how often we would see a treatment effect estimate of this size just by chance, under the assumption that individual treatment effects were actually zero.
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Key Fisher move: hold outcomes fixed, vary only the random assignment.
\item Under the sharp null ($\tau_i=0$ for all $i$), every unit's outcome is the same under treatment and control, so only $W$ changes across re-randomizations.
\item Then the distribution of the estimator under re-randomization is known.
\end{itemize}

}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Sharp null hypothesis of no effect}

Assuming all treatment effects are exactly zero is called the \textbf{sharp null hypothesis of no effect.} \pause Also referred to as ``Fisher's null'' after Sir Ronald Aylmer Fisher (1890-1962).

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth,height=0.7\textheight,keepaspectratio]{../assets/youngronaldfisher.jpeg}
\end{figure}


\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Define ``sharp'' null: specifies the missing potential outcomes for every unit (so we can impute $Y_i(0)$ and $Y_i(1)$).
\item Contrast with ``weak'' null like $\E[\tau_i]=0$, which does \emph{not} tell us each individual $\tau_i$.
\item Historical note: Fisher framed inference around the randomization itself.
\end{itemize}

}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Sharp null hypothesis of no effect}

We might write the sharp null hypothesis this way:
$$
\text{H}_0:\tau_i = 0, \text{ for all } i \text{ in our pop.}
$$
This implies that potential outcomes are identical under treatment and control, for all individuals.
$$
Y_i(0) = Y_i(1)
$$

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Under $H_0$, $Y_i(0)=Y_i(1)$ for all $i$; therefore the observed $Y_i$ is the same no matter what assignment we imagine.
\item This is what lets us construct the full set of potential outcomes needed for a randomization test.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Sharp null hypothesis of no effect}

\begin{itemize}
\item To test this hypothesis, we also need to know (or assume we know) the randomization procedure\pause
\item Here, we'll assume exactly two villages are assigned treatment. 
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item To get a null distribution, we need the assignment mechanism: here, exactly 2 treated out of 7 (complete randomization with fixed treated count).
\item That pins down the set of possible $W$ vectors and their probabilities.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Chattopadhyay \& Duflo example}
Then we can fill in this table\dots

\begin{table}[]
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
& $Y_i(0)$ & $Y_i(1)$ & $W_i$ & $Y_i$ & $\tau_i$ \\
Village 1 & \textcolor{red}{?} & 15 & 1 & 15 & \textcolor{red}{?} \\
Village 2 & 15 & \textcolor{red}{?} & 0 & 15 & \textcolor{red}{?} \\
Village 3 & 20 & \textcolor{red}{?} & 0 & 20 & \textcolor{red}{?} \\
Village 4 & 20 & \textcolor{red}{?} & 0 & 20 & \textcolor{red}{?} \\
Village 5 & 10 & \textcolor{red}{?} & 0 & 10 & \textcolor{red}{?} \\
Village 6 & 15 & \textcolor{red}{?} & 0 & 15 & \textcolor{red}{?} \\
Village 7 & \textcolor{red}{?} & 30 & 1 & 30 & \textcolor{red}{?}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Under the sharp null, the missing counterfactuals are just the observed outcomes copied over.
\item Intuition: if treatment has no effect on anyone, the outcome we saw would have happened regardless of assignment.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Chattopadhyay \& Duflo example}
\dots as this:

\begin{table}[]
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
& $Y_i(0)$ & $Y_i(1)$ & $W_i$ & $Y_i$ &$\tau_i$ \\
Village 1 & \textcolor{red}{15} & 15 & 1 & 15 & \textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 2 & 15 & \textcolor{red}{15} & 0 & 15 & \textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 3 & 20 & \textcolor{red}{20} & 0 & 20 & \textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 4 & 20 & \textcolor{red}{20} & 0 & 20 & \textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 5 & 10 & \textcolor{red}{10} & 0 & 10 & \textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 6 & 15 & \textcolor{red}{15} & 0 & 15 & \textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 7 & \textcolor{red}{30} & 30 & 1 & 30 & \textcolor{red}{0}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Now every $\tau_i$ is 0 by construction, but the \emph{estimate} $\hat\tau$ can still vary with assignment.
\item This is the key distinction: ``no true effect'' does not imply ``we always estimate 0'' in finite samples.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Gerber Green example}
We can re-run the randomization, and the potential outcomes and observed $Y_i$ will not change, but the treatment effect estimate will. 

\begin{table}[]
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
& $Y_i(0)$ & $Y_i(1)$ & $W_i$ & $Y_i$ & $\tau_i$ \\
Village 1 & \textcolor{red}{15} & 15 & \textcolor{blue}{0} & 15 & \textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 2 & 15 & \textcolor{red}{15} & \textcolor{blue}{1} & 15 &\textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 3 & 20 & \textcolor{red}{20} & \textcolor{blue}{0} & 20 &\textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 4 & 20 & \textcolor{red}{20} & \textcolor{blue}{0} & 20 &\textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 5 & 10 & \textcolor{red}{10} & \textcolor{blue}{0} & 10 &\textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 6 & 15 & \textcolor{red}{15} & \textcolor{blue}{1} & 15 &\textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 7 & \textcolor{red}{30} & 30 & \textcolor{blue}{0} & 30 &\textcolor{red}{0}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}
\pause
$$
\bar{Y}(1) = 15 \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \bar{Y}(0) = 19
$$
\pause
$$
\color{blue}{\hat{\tau} = 15-19 = \bm{-4}}
$$

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Re-randomize $W$ while holding the $Y$'s fixed (sharp null world).
\item Compute the same estimator each time; watch how much it can vary even when the null is true.
\item We are building the randomization (null) distribution of $\hat\tau_{DM}$.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Gerber Green example}
We can re-run the randomization, and the potential outcomes and observed $Y_i$ will not change, but the treatment effect estimate will. 

\begin{table}[]
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
& $Y_i(0)$ & $Y_i(1)$ & $W_i$ & $Y_i$ & $\tau_i$ \\
Village 1 & \textcolor{red}{15} & 15 & \textcolor{blue}{0} & 15 & \textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 2 & 15 & \textcolor{red}{15} & \textcolor{blue}{0} & 15 &\textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 3 & 20 & \textcolor{red}{20} & \textcolor{blue}{1} & 20 &\textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 4 & 20 & \textcolor{red}{20} & \textcolor{blue}{1} & 20 &\textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 5 & 10 & \textcolor{red}{10} & \textcolor{blue}{0} & 10 &\textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 6 & 15 & \textcolor{red}{15} & \textcolor{blue}{0} & 15 &\textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 7 & \textcolor{red}{30} & 30 & \textcolor{blue}{0} & 30 &\textcolor{red}{0}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}
\pause
$$
\bar{Y}(1) = 20 \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \bar{Y}(0) = 17
$$
\pause
$$
\color{blue}{\hat{\tau} = 20-17= \bm{3}}
$$

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Another re-randomization: now $\hat\tau_{DM}=3$.
\item Point: even under the sharp null, we can see estimates that look ``non-zero'' just from chance imbalance.
\item We need to compare our observed estimate to this reference distribution.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Gerber Green example}
We can re-run the randomization, and the potential outcomes and observed $Y_i$ will not change, but the treatment effect estimate will. 

\begin{table}[]
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
& $Y_i(0)$ & $Y_i(1)$ & $W_i$ & $Y_i$ & $\tau_i$ \\
Village 1 & \textcolor{red}{15} & 15 & \textcolor{blue}{0} & 15 & \textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 2 & 15 & \textcolor{red}{15} & \textcolor{blue}{0} & 15 &\textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 3 & 20 & \textcolor{red}{20} & \textcolor{blue}{1} & 20 &\textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 4 & 20 & \textcolor{red}{20} & \textcolor{blue}{0} & 20 &\textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 5 & 10 & \textcolor{red}{10} & \textcolor{blue}{0} & 10 &\textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 6 & 15 & \textcolor{red}{15} & \textcolor{blue}{0} & 15 &\textcolor{red}{0} \\
Village 7 & \textcolor{red}{30} & 30 & \textcolor{blue}{1} & 30 &\textcolor{red}{0}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}
\pause
$$
\bar{Y}(1) = 25 \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \bar{Y}(0) = 15
$$
\pause
$$
\color{blue}{\hat{\tau} = 25-15 = \bm{10}}
$$

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Yet another re-randomization: now $\hat\tau_{DM}=10$.
\item This illustrates that ``large'' estimates are possible under the null in very small samples.
\item The p-value answers: how often do we see estimates at least this extreme under the null?
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}

\begin{itemize}
\item Because we know how treatment was assigned, we know all the possible ways treatment could be assigned across the villages, and the exact probability.\pause
\item There are seven villages, and we say that exactly two will get treatment. Each village is assigned treatment with equal probability.

\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item The key ingredient is the known assignment rule: ``choose exactly 2 of 7 for treatment''.
\item Because the assignment mechanism is known, we can enumerate (or simulate) what would happen under re-randomization.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]
We can use the package \texttt{ri} to find the \textit{exact} distribution of $\hat\tau_{DM}$ under the sharp null. \\~\

Our real data:
\scriptsize
<<>>=
df <- data.frame(
        # our initial treatment vector
        W = c(1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1),
        # our initial response vector
        Y = c(15, 15, 20, 20, 10, 15, 30),
        # treatment assignment probability
        probs = rep(2/7, 7)
)

df
@



\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item This is the observed dataset: a treatment indicator $W$ and an outcome $Y$ for each of 7 villages.
\item We'll treat the potential outcomes as fixed and use the assignment mechanism to generate the null distribution.
\item Because the sample is tiny, we can enumerate all assignments (an ``exact'' randomization test).
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]


And our difference in means estimate of the average treatment effect under the real data:

<<>>=
Y1 <- df$Y[which(df$W == 1)]
Y0 <- df$Y[which(df$W == 0)]

(dm_hat <- mean(Y1) - mean(Y0))
@


\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Compute the observed difference-in-means estimate $\hat\tau_{DM}$ from the realized assignment.
\item We'll compare this value (6.5) to what we'd typically see under the sharp null.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]
Adding in the hypothetical data. 

<<>>=
df <-  cbind( # binds the columns together
        df,
        # Y(0) under the sharp null of no effect
        Y0 = df$Y,
        # Y(1) under the sharp null of no effect
        Y1 = df$Y)

df
@


\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Under the sharp null, $Y_i(0)=Y_i(1)=Y_i$; so we can ``fill in'' both potential outcomes by copying the observed $Y$.
\item This step is what makes the randomization distribution computable.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]
Consider all the ways treatment could be assigned: (this is what \texttt{ri} is doing for us)

\scriptsize
<<>>=
(perms <- genperms(df$W))
@

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Enumerate all possible treatment vectors consistent with the design (exactly 2 treated out of 7).
\item There are ${7 \\choose 2}=21$ possible assignments.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]
Then generate the sampling distribution of the ATE estimate under the sharp null of no effect.
\small

<<>>=
Ys_null <- list(
        Y0 = df$Y0,
        Y1 = df$Y1
)

dm <- gendist(Ys_null,
              perms, 
              prob=df$probs)
dm
@

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item For each possible assignment, compute the same estimator $\hat\tau_{DM}$.
\item The resulting list is the \emph{exact} null distribution of the estimator under Fisher's sharp null.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%

\begin{frame}[fragile]

The mean under our null distribution is \textit{exactly} zero. Why?

<<>>=
mean(dm)
@


\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Under complete randomization and the sharp null, the randomization distribution of $\hat\tau_{DM}$ is centered at 0.
\item Intuition: treatment labels are unrelated to outcomes under $H_0$, so positive and negative differences balance out.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]

\begin{center}
<<fig.width = 5, fig.height =5, echo=FALSE, out.width='0.6\\textwidth'>>=
gg_bins <- aggregate(list(rf = dm), by = list(dm = dm), length)
gg_bins$col <- abs(gg_bins$dm) >= dm_hat

ggplot(gg_bins, aes(x = dm, y = rf)) +
        geom_col() +
        geom_vline(xintercept = 0, color = 'red') +
        xlab('Difference in means estimates') + 
        ylab('Frequency') +
        ggtitle('Distribution of difference in means estimates under\nthe sharp null')
@
\end{center}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item This bar chart is the randomization distribution of $\hat\tau_{DM}$ under the sharp null.
\item Red line at 0: where we'd expect the estimate to be centered if the null is true.
\item Next step: locate our observed estimate (6.5) and compute how ``extreme'' it is relative to this distribution.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]
 \scriptsize
<<>>=
prop.table(table(dm))
(pval <- mean(abs(dm) >= dm_hat))
@


\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item The two-tailed p-value here is the share of assignments that produce an estimate at least as extreme as 6.5 in absolute value.
\item With only 21 possible assignments, we can literally count them (exact p-value).
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%

\begin{frame}{p-value}

\begin{itemize}
\item Under the null distribution, if we were to re-randomize the experiment many times, we would see a value \textit{at least as extreme as our estimate} \Sexpr{ round(pval*100,2)}\% of the time.\pause
\item That doesn't seem \textbf{that} unlikely--more than one in three times, we would see an estimate as large as we got, just by chance. 
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Interpretation: under $H_0$, about 38\% of re-randomizations would look at least this extreme.
\item Emphasize: p-value is about the \emph{distribution under the null}, not ``probability the null is true''.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]

\texttt{ri} produces the same exact p-value.


<<>>=
dispdist(distout = dm, 
         ate = dm_hat, 
         display.plot = FALSE)$two.tailed.p.value.abs
@

\pause
That's because it is doing the exact same thing under the hood. 


\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Sanity check: \texttt{dispdist()} is just automating what we did (enumerate assignments, compute statistic, compare to observed).
\item Use it to reinforce that inference is coming from the design (randomization), not from large-sample approximations.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}

\begin{itemize}
\item Why is this test called an ``exact'' test?\pause
\item Because we know the \textit{exact} distribution of our estimate under the specified null. We do not have to approximate the distribution.
\item This will not be true for all of our hypothesis testing\dots
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item ``Exact'' because we enumerated the full randomization distribution, not a simulation and not a normal approximation.
\item Preview: when the number of assignments explodes, we'll approximate by simulation (randomization inference via Monte Carlo).
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Hypotheses}

\begin{itemize}
\item We framed our null hypothesis as below:

$$
H_0: \tau_i = 0, \text{ for all } i \text{ in our pop.}
$$

\pause
\item Implicitly, the alternative is that for some individual(s), the treatment effect is non-zero. 

$$
H_A: \tau_i \neq 0, \text{ for some } i \text{ in our pop.}
$$

\pause

\item In our case, we did not find strong evidence to reject the null hypothesis, i.e., our data is consistent with what we would see if the null hypothesis were true.

\pause

\item Note that we do NOT say that we reject or accept the alternative hypothesis.

\pause

\item We can only say that our results were not consistent with or were consistent with what we would have seen under the null--i.e., we have evidence to reject or fail to reject the null. 
\end{itemize}


\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Be explicit about what $H_0$ and $H_A$ mean in potential-outcomes terms.
\item Decision language: reject vs fail to reject; never ``accept $H_0$'' in the usual NHST framing.
\item Here, large p-value $\Rightarrow$ data are not surprising under the null.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Distributions of estimators}

\begin{itemize}
\item Our difference in means estimator is a function of the data we observe. \pause
\item Because there is randomness in the data, here, due to random assignment of treatment, the estimator is also a random variable.\pause
\item Just like other random variables have distributions to describe them, estimators also have distributions.\pause
\item We don't know the \textit{true} distribution of the difference in means estimator, for the same reason that we don't know individual treatment effects. \textcolor{red}{(FPoCI!)}\pause
\item But we DO know what the distribution would be under the null. 
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item The estimator is a random variable because $W$ is random (by design) and therefore the computed $\hat\tau$ varies across realizations.
\item We cannot observe the ``true'' sampling distribution in general, but we can characterize it under a specified null.
\item This is the core logic of Fisher randomization tests.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}

Note that we are conducting inference with respect to: \pause

\begin{itemize}
\item a defined population \pause
\item a defined treatment \pause
\item a defined outcome \pause
\item a known treatment assignment mechanism \pause
\item a given estimating procedure
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Scope conditions matter: what population, what treatment, what outcome, what assignment mechanism.
\item If any of these change, the estimand or the relevant randomization distribution changes too.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{The null}

\begin{itemize}
\item How do we determine what the null is?\pause
\item We formalize our hypotheses in terms of the effect we are trying to find in the data. Is there a treatment effect? Is there a difference between these two groups?\pause
\item The null is (often, but not always) the case when there is no effect, or no difference.\pause
\item We can imagine other kinds of hypotheses, for example that effects are bounded away from zero and positive, or exactly .2. And we can characterize the distribution of our test statistic under the null. 
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item The null is a formal statement about the world that implies a distribution for a chosen test statistic.
\item In experiments, a common null is ``no effect''; but you can test other nulls (e.g., effect size equals 0.2, or effect is nonnegative).
\item Always connect the null to the substantive theory you are trying to learn about.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{The null}

\begin{itemize}
\item Is the sharp null of no individual level effects plausible in this setting? \pause
\item Does it matter?
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Sharp null is often unrealistic (effects can vary), but it is a clean starting point for understanding randomization tests.
\item If we reject the sharp null, we learn ``some effect exists for some units''; if we fail to reject, we have limited evidence, especially in small samples.
\item Later we'll discuss other inferential targets beyond the sharp null.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%

\begin{frame}[c]{Getting to some real data\dots}

\Large
\centering
\textcolor{Maroon}{Butler, Daniel M., \& Broockman, David E. (2011). Do politicians racially discriminate against constituents? A field experiment on state legislators.}\\~\

Data is available at the Yale ISPS data archive: \\~\
\url{isps.yale.edu/research/data}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Transition from toy example to a real field experiment dataset.
\item Goal: same Fisher logic, but with a huge number of possible assignments (so we approximate rather than enumerate).
\end{itemize}

}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%

\begin{frame}[fragile]{Loading the data}

\scriptsize

<<load>>=
df <- read.csv('../data/butler-broockman.csv', as.is = TRUE)
head(df)
@



\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Load the Butler--Broockman field experiment data (audit emails to legislators).
\item Point out: this is \emph{experimental} data (treatment randomized), so we can do design-based inference.
\end{itemize}

}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]{Examining the data}
\scriptsize
<<summary>>=
str(df)
@


\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Walk through variable names: treatment indicators for sender name, outcomes for reply.
\item Emphasize unit of analysis: legislator-email contact.
\end{itemize}

}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Data}

\begin{itemize}
\item Where does it come from/how is it generated?\pause
\item What is the sample population? \pause
\item What is being measured? 
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}

%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Source: randomized audit study; treatment is the perceived race signaled by the sender name.
\item Population for inference here is the set of legislators contacted in the experiment; the randomness comes from assignment, not sampling.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]


Recall that treatment is 1 if the sender was DeShawn Jackson, and 0 if Jake Mueller. 

<<>>=
table(df$treat_deshawn)
@


\pause

The primary outcome is whether legislators replied at all. 

<<>>=
table(df$reply_atall)
@

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Check balance: treatment assigned to about half the contacts (good sign randomization worked as intended).
\item Outcome is binary reply; we will use difference-in-means as the estimand for reply rate differences.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]

We're going to manipulate our data so it takes the format $Y$ $\sim$ $W$. 


<<>>=
df$W <- df$treat_deshawn
df$Y <- df$reply_atall
@


\pause

To get the difference-in-means estimate of the ATE, 

<<>>=
Y1 <- df$Y[which(df$W == 1)]
Y0 <- df$Y[which(df$W == 0)]
@


\pause

<<>>=
(dm_hat <- mean(Y1) - mean(Y0))
@

\pause
Legislators were 1.7 percentage points less likely to reply to an email if the sender was identified as DeShawn Jackson as compared to Jake Mueller. 

\end{frame}

%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Compute $\hat\tau_{DM}=\bar Y_{W=1}-\bar Y_{W=0}$: difference in reply rates.
\item Interpret sign and magnitude (about -1.8 percentage points).
\item Next: is this difference bigger than what we'd expect just by chance under Fisher's null?
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]

Note that again, the population that we're taking inference over is legislators--all of whom are included in our experiment. We're not assuming we're sampling from some other distribution. The only source of randomness is how treatment is assigned. 

\end{frame}

%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Stress design-based perspective: we are not assuming i.i.d.\ sampling from a superpopulation.
\item The source of uncertainty is the randomized assignment of treatment labels.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]

\begin{itemize}
\item There are ${4859 \choose 2428}$ different ways treatment could be assigned--this is too many to generate the whole matrix of permutations and get the \textit{exact} sampling distribution. \pause
\item Instead, we'll simulate the sampling process many times to find the \textit{approximate} sampling distribution of $\hat\tau_{DM}$ under the sharp null. \pause
\end{itemize}


<<>>=
# randomization inference function
my_ri <- function(df){
        df_ri <- df
        df_ri$newW <- sample(df$W)
        Y1_ri <- df$Y[which(df_ri$newW == 1)]
        Y0_ri <- df$Y[which(df_ri$newW == 0)]
        ate_hat <- mean(Y1_ri)-mean(Y0_ri)
  return(ate_hat)
}

@


\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item With ${4859 \\choose 2428}$ assignments, exact enumeration is impossible.
\item Monte Carlo randomization inference: repeatedly shuffle $W$ (keeping the treated count fixed) and recompute the statistic.
\item \texttt{my\_ri()} is the ``one re-randomization'' function; \texttt{replicate()} will call it many times.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]
Let's try it. \pause

<<>>=
my_ri(df)
@

\pause
And again. 
<<>>=
my_ri(df)
@


\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Each call to \texttt{my\_ri()} shuffles treatment and gives a new ``placebo'' estimate under the sharp null.
\item These values should fluctuate around 0; if the observed estimate is in the tails, that's evidence against $H_0$.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]

We can do this many times to find the distribution of $\hat\tau_{DM}$ under the sharp null. 

\scriptsize
<<>>=
# number of iterations
n_iter <- 1000

# replicate does the same (random) function many times
dm <- replicate(n = n_iter, my_ri(df))
head(dm)
@


\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item We approximate the randomization distribution with many shuffles (here 1000).
\item More iterations $\Rightarrow$ more stable approximation of the p-value, but computation cost rises.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]


\begin{center}
<<fig.width = 5, fig.height =5, echo=FALSE, out.width='0.6\\textwidth'>>=
null_dist <- data.frame(dm)
null_dist$bins <- cut(dm, breaks = 50)
null_dist <- aggregate(list(count = null_dist$dm), by = list(bins = null_dist$bins), length)
null_dist$bin_max <- as.numeric(gsub(".?(-?[0-9]+[.]+[0-9]+).*", "\\1", null_dist$bins))
null_dist$bin_min <- as.numeric(gsub(".*,(-?[0-9]+[.]+[0-9]+(e-[0-9]+)?)]$", "\\1", null_dist$bins))
null_dist$bin_mid <- (null_dist$bin_max - null_dist$bin_min)/2 + null_dist$bin_min
null_dist$col <- abs(null_dist$bin_min) >= abs(dm_hat)

ggplot(null_dist, aes(x = bin_mid, y = count)) +
  geom_col() +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 0, color = 'red')
@
\end{center}

\pause

<<echo = FALSE, include = FALSE>>=
pval_twotailed <- mean(abs(dm) >= abs(dm_hat))
@

The two-tailed p-value is the probability mass in both tails beyond the observed estimate.

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Histogram of simulated null distribution of $\hat\tau_{DM}$ from shuffling $W$.
\item Compute two-tailed p-value: fraction of simulated estimates with $|\hat\tau|\ge|\hat\tau^{obs}|$.
\item Interpretation: how surprising is our observed difference under Fisher's null?
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]


\begin{center}
<<fig.width = 5, fig.height =5, echo=FALSE, out.width='0.6\\textwidth'>>=

ggplot(null_dist, aes(x = bin_mid, y = count, fill = col)) +
  geom_col() +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 0, color = 'red') + 
  scale_fill_manual(values=c("grey35", "#619CFF")) +
  theme(legend.position = 'none')
@
\end{center}

\pause

\[
p = \Pr_0[|\hat{\tau}| \ge |\hat{\tau}^{obs}|] \approx \Sexpr{round(pval_twotailed, 3)}
\]


\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Same idea, but now the ``as extreme as observed'' bins are highlighted.
\item Visually reinforces what the p-value is counting in a two-tailed test.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]

The types of hypotheses we've considered are two-sided hypotheses: we look at effects in either direction from zero. 

\pause

$$
H_0: \tau_i = 0, \text{ for all } i \text{ in our pop.}
$$


$$
H_A: \tau_i \neq 0, \text{ for some } i \text{ in our pop.}
$$

\end{frame}

%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Two-sided alternatives ask: is there evidence the effect is different from 0 in \emph{either} direction?
\item The corresponding p-value counts estimates in both tails (by absolute value).
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]

We can also consider other alternative hypotheses. For example, the hypothesis that treatment effects are less than zero. This is called a one-sided hypothesis. 


\pause

$$
H_0: \tau_i = 0, \text{ for all } i \text{ in our pop.}
$$


$$
H_A: \tau_i < 0, \text{ for some } i \text{ in our pop.}
$$

\end{frame}

%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item One-sided alternatives are appropriate when theory implies a direction (e.g., effect should be negative).
\item One-sided p-value counts only the tail in the hypothesized direction (here, left tail).
\item Caution: choosing one- vs two-sided should be decided \emph{before} looking at results.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]

\begin{itemize}
\item The alternative hypothesis that we consider is a consequence of the social science theory we're trying to test. \pause
\item Here, we want to see if legislators are \textit{less likely} to respond to a constituent named DeShawn Jackson, as compared to Jake Mueller.  \pause
\item When we test a one-sided hypothesis, we want to check how likely we would be to observe statistics at least as large as the test statistic that we actually observe, in the direction of our hypothesis. 
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}

%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Connect to substantive theory: here, discrimination story implies lower reply rates for the treatment name.
\item Translate to test-statistic language: ``more negative than expected under the null''.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]


\begin{center}
<<fig.width = 5, fig.height =5, echo=FALSE, out.width='0.6\\textwidth'>>=
null_dist$col2 <- null_dist$bin_min <= dm_hat

ggplot(null_dist, aes(x = bin_mid, y = count)) +
  geom_col() +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 0, color = 'red') + 
  scale_fill_manual(values=c("grey35", "#619CFF")) +
  theme(legend.position = 'none')
@
\end{center}

\pause

<<echo = FALSE, include = FALSE>>=
pval_lower <- mean(dm <= dm_hat)
@

The lower-tail p-value is the probability mass to the left of the observed estimate under the null.

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Build the one-sided (lower-tail) p-value: how often does the shuffled estimate fall \emph{below} the observed estimate?
\item Visually, this is the left tail beyond $\hat\\tau^{obs}$.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]


\begin{center}
<<fig.width = 5, fig.height =5, echo=FALSE, out.width='0.55\\textwidth'>>=
ggplot(null_dist, aes(x = bin_mid, y = count, fill = col2)) +
  geom_col() +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 0, color = 'red') + 
  scale_fill_manual(values=c("grey35", "#619CFF")) +
  theme(legend.position = 'none')
@
\end{center}


\[
p = \Pr_0[\hat{\tau} \le \hat{\tau}^{obs}] \approx \Sexpr{round(pval_lower, 3)}
\]


\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Shaded bins correspond to the probability mass counted in the one-sided p-value.
\item Use this to reinforce the definition: a p-value is always ``probability under the null of something at least as extreme''.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]{P-values}


Suppose $\hat{\theta}$ is the general form for an estimate produced by our estimator, and $\hat{\theta}^*$ is the value we have actually observed. 

\end{frame}

%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item We now generalize beyond this example: define p-values in terms of a generic statistic $\\hat\\theta$ and its null distribution.
\item The key object is the probability distribution under $H_0$ (from randomization, or later from approximations).
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]{P-values}

\begin{itemize}
\item A lower one-tailed p-value under the null hypothesis is 
$$
p = \Pr_0[\hat{\theta} \le \hat{\theta}^*]
$$
\end{itemize}
i.e., the probability \textit{under the null distribution} that we would see an estimate of $\hat{\theta}$ that is less than or equal to what we saw from the data. 

\pause
\begin{figure}
\centering
\resizebox{0.4\textwidth}{!}{
<<fig.width = 5, fig.height =5, echo=FALSE>>=
ggplot(null_dist, aes(x = bin_mid, y = count, fill = col2)) +
  geom_col() +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 0, color = 'red') + 
  scale_fill_manual(values=c("grey35", "#619CFF")) +
  theme(legend.position = 'none')
@
}
\end{figure}

\end{frame}

%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Lower one-tailed p-value: evidence for negative effects / unusually small statistics.
\item Emphasize the subscript 0: probability is evaluated under the null.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]{P-values}

\begin{itemize}
\item An upper one-tailed p-value under the null hypothesis is 

$$
p = \Pr_0[\hat{\theta} \ge \hat{\theta}^*]
$$
\end{itemize}
i.e., the probability \textit{under the null distribution} that we would see an estimate of $\hat{\theta}$ that is greater than or equal to what we saw from the data. 

\pause
\begin{figure}
\centering
\resizebox{0.4\textwidth}{!}{
<<fig.width = 5, fig.height =5, echo=FALSE>>=
null_dist$col3 <- null_dist$bin_min >= dm_hat
ggplot(null_dist, aes(x = bin_mid, y = count, fill = col3)) +
  geom_col() +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 0, color = 'red') + 
  scale_fill_manual(values=c("grey35", "#619CFF")) +
  theme(legend.position = 'none')
@
}
\end{figure}

\end{frame}

%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Upper one-tailed p-value: evidence for positive effects / unusually large statistics.
\item Same idea, opposite tail.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}[fragile]{P-values}

\begin{itemize}
\item A two-tailed p-value under the null hypothesis is 
$$
p = \Pr_0[|\hat{\theta}| \ge |\hat{\theta}^*|]
$$
\end{itemize}

i.e., the probability \textit{under the null distribution} that we would see an estimate of $\hat{\theta}$ as or more extreme as what we saw from the data. 
\pause
\begin{figure}
\centering
\resizebox{0.4\textwidth}{!}{
<<fig.width = 5, fig.height =5, echo=FALSE>>=
ggplot(null_dist, aes(x = bin_mid, y = count, fill = col)) +
  geom_col() +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 0, color = 'red') + 
  scale_fill_manual(values=c("grey35", "#619CFF")) +
  theme(legend.position = 'none')
@
}
\end{figure}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item Two-tailed p-value counts extremeness in both directions (absolute value).
\item Appropriate when theory does not specify direction, or when deviations in either direction matter.
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

